IMPORT prelude

-- International Arbitration Act 1994 (Singapore) -- when the Act's Part 2 and the Model Law govern an arbitration (ss 5, 15, 26),
-- when an arbitration agreement is "in writing" (s 2A), the mandatory stay of court proceedings (s 6), the default number and
-- appointment of arbitrators (ss 9, 9A), the 30-day window to challenge a jurisdiction ruling (s 10(3)), the limit on security for
-- costs against foreign claimants (s 12(4)), setting aside an award (s 24 and Model Law Article 34) and refusing enforcement of a
-- foreign award (s 31).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/IAA1994.txt, with amendments to
-- Act 5 of 2025 (in force 9 March 2025) shown. The deposit labels itself "Current version as at 01 Oct 2026". The arrangement of
-- sections at the top of the deposit is out of step with the body (it lists "22. Court may set aside award", "23. Liability of
-- arbitrator"); this encoding follows the BODY, where setting aside is s 24 and arbitrator liability s 25.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 6 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the questions a party or practitioner
-- meets first: does the Act apply, is there a written agreement, must the court stay, how many arbitrators and who appoints them,
-- and what grounds and deadlines govern attacking or resisting an award. Not encoded: tribunal powers beyond s 12(4) (s 12(1)-(6)),
-- court-ordered interim measures (s 12A), orders to attend (s 13), conciliation (ss 16, 17), interest and costs (ss 20, 21),
-- privacy of court proceedings and reporting (ss 22, 23), immunities (ss 25, 25A), the IPR Part (Part 2A), the limitation rules
-- (s 8A), the three-party default appointment (s 9B), and the Model Law (First Schedule) beyond Article 34.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `International Arbitration Act 1994 -- arbitration`

§§ `5, 15, 26 -- when Part 2 and the Model Law apply`

-- s 5(1): "This Part and the Model Law do not apply to an arbitration which is not an international arbitration unless the parties
-- agree in writing that this Part or the Model Law is to apply".
-- s 5(2): "Despite Article 1(3) of the Model Law, an arbitration is international if -- (a) at least one of the parties ... at the
-- time of the conclusion of the agreement, has its place of business in any State other than Singapore; (b) one of the following
-- places is situated outside the State in which the parties have their places of business: (i) the place of arbitration ...;
-- (ii) any place where a substantial part of the obligations ... is to be performed or the place with which the subject matter of
-- the dispute is most closely connected; or (c) the parties have expressly agreed that the subject matter ... relates to more than
-- one country."
-- (Model Law Article 1(3)(a), which s 5(2) displaces, asks instead whether the parties have their places of business "in different
-- States".)
-- s 15(1): if the parties "have expressly agreed either (a) that the Model Law or this Part is not to apply ...; or (b) that the
-- Arbitration Act 2001 ... is to apply", then the Model Law and Part 2 do not apply. s 15(2): "a provision in an arbitration
-- agreement referring to or adopting any rules of arbitration is not of itself sufficient to exclude" them.
-- s 26(1): Part 2 does not apply to an international arbitration "commenced before 27 January 1995 unless the parties have (whether
-- in the agreement or in any other document in writing) otherwise agreed".
DECLARE Arbitration HAS
    `a party had its place of business outside Singapore when the agreement was concluded` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seat is outside the parties' State` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the main place of performance or closest connection is outside the parties' State` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parties expressly agreed the subject matter relates to more than one country` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parties agreed in writing that this Part or the Model Law applies` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parties expressly excluded the Model Law or this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parties expressly chose the Arbitration Act 2001` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the agreement adopts institutional rules of arbitration` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was commenced before 27 January 1995` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the arbitration` IS AN Arbitration
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the arbitration is international` IF
        `the arbitration`'s `a party had its place of business outside Singapore when the agreement was concluded`
    OR  `the arbitration`'s `the seat is outside the parties' State`
    OR  `the arbitration`'s `the main place of performance or closest connection is outside the parties' State`
    OR  `the arbitration`'s `the parties expressly agreed the subject matter relates to more than one country`

-- The institutional-rules flag is deliberately not consulted: s 15(2) says adopting rules does not of itself exclude Part 2.
GIVEN `the arbitration` IS AN Arbitration
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Part 2 and the Model Law apply` IF
        (`the arbitration is international` `the arbitration` OR `the arbitration`'s `the parties agreed in writing that this Part or the Model Law applies`)
    AND (NOT `the arbitration`'s `the parties expressly excluded the Model Law or this Part`)
    AND (NOT `the arbitration`'s `the parties expressly chose the Arbitration Act 2001`)
    AND (    (NOT `the arbitration`'s `it was commenced before 27 January 1995`)
         OR  `the arbitration`'s `the parties agreed in writing that this Part or the Model Law applies`)

§§ `2A -- an arbitration agreement in writing`

-- s 2A(3): "An arbitration agreement must be in writing." (4): it "is in writing if its content is recorded in any form, whether or
-- not the arbitration agreement or contract has been concluded orally, by conduct or by other means." (5): satisfied by an
-- electronic communication "accessible so as to be useable for subsequent reference". (6): an assertion of an arbitration agreement
-- in a pleading, calling for a reply, that "is not denied" -- there "is deemed to be an effective arbitration agreement". (7): a
-- reference in a contract to a document containing an arbitration clause, if it makes that clause part of the contract.
-- How the agreement was concluded (orally, by conduct) is deliberately not an input: s 2A(4) makes it irrelevant.
GIVEN `its content is recorded in some form` IS A BOOLEAN, `it is in an accessible electronic communication` IS A BOOLEAN, `it was asserted in a pleading and not denied` IS A BOOLEAN, `the contract incorporates a document with an arbitration clause` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the arbitration agreement is in writing` IF
        `its content is recorded in some form`
    OR  `it is in an accessible electronic communication`
    OR  `it was asserted in a pleading and not denied`
    OR  `the contract incorporates a document with an arbitration clause`

§§ `6 -- stay of court proceedings`

-- s 6(1): a party may, "at any time after filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest and before delivering
-- any pleading (other than a pleading asserting that the court does not have jurisdiction in the proceedings) or taking any other
-- step in the proceedings, apply to that court to stay the proceedings". (2): the court "is to make an order ... staying the
-- proceedings ... unless it is satisfied that the arbitration agreement is null and void, inoperative or incapable of being
-- performed."
GIVEN `filed and served a notice of intention to contest` IS A BOOLEAN, `delivered a pleading on the merits` IS A BOOLEAN, `took another step in the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN, `the agreement is null and void, inoperative or incapable of being performed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court must stay the proceedings` IF
        `filed and served a notice of intention to contest`
    AND (NOT `delivered a pleading on the merits`)
    AND (NOT `took another step in the proceedings`)
    AND (NOT `the agreement is null and void, inoperative or incapable of being performed`)

§§ `9, 9A -- number and appointment of arbitrators`

-- s 9: "Despite Article 10(2) of the Model Law, if the number of arbitrators is not determined by the parties, there is to be a
-- single arbitrator." (Here an agreed number of 0 means the parties did not determine one.)
GIVEN `the number agreed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of arbitrators` MEANS
    IF `the number agreed` EQUALS 0 THEN 1 ELSE `the number agreed`

-- s 9A(1): with 2 parties and 3 arbitrators, each party appoints one and the parties agree the third. (2): "Where the parties fail to
-- agree on the appointment of the third arbitrator within 30 days after the receipt of the first request by either party to do so,
-- the appointment must be made, upon the request of a party, by the appointing authority." s 8(2): the appointing authority is the
-- president of the Court of Arbitration of the Singapore International Arbitration Centre.
GIVEN `days since the first request was received` IS A NUMBER, `the parties agreed on the third arbitrator` IS A BOOLEAN, `a party asked the appointing authority` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appointing authority must appoint the third arbitrator` IF
        (NOT `the parties agreed on the third arbitrator`)
    AND `days since the first request was received` GREATER THAN 30
    AND `a party asked the appointing authority`

§§ `10(3) -- challenging a jurisdiction ruling`

-- s 10(3): if the tribunal rules on a plea "as a preliminary question that it has jurisdiction" or "at any stage ... that it has no
-- jurisdiction", "any party may, within 30 days after having received notice of that ruling, apply to the General Division of the
-- High Court to decide the matter."
GIVEN `days since notice of the ruling` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a challenge to the jurisdiction ruling is in time` IF
    `days since notice of the ruling` AT MOST 30

§§ `12(4) -- security for costs`

-- s 12(4): the power to order a claimant to provide security for costs "must not be exercised by reason only that the claimant is
-- (a) an individual ordinarily resident outside Singapore; or (b) a corporation or an association incorporated or formed under the
-- law of a country outside Singapore, or whose central management and control is exercised outside Singapore."
DECLARE `Security Reason` IS ONE OF
    `the claimant lives abroad`
    `the claimant is a foreign company`
    `the claimant appears unable to pay costs`

GIVEN `the reason` IS A `Security Reason`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 12(4) bars security on that reason alone` IF
    CONSIDER `the reason`
    WHEN `the claimant lives abroad` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the claimant is a foreign company` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `24 and Model Law Article 34 -- setting aside an award`

-- Model Law Article 34(1): "Recourse to a court against an arbitral award may be made only by an application for setting aside";
-- (2): "may be set aside ... only if" the applicant proves (i) incapacity or an invalid agreement, (ii) no proper notice or inability
-- to present the case, (iii) decisions beyond the submission, (iv) composition or procedure not as agreed; or the court finds (b)(i)
-- the subject matter not arbitrable, (ii) the award in conflict with public policy.
-- s 24: "in addition to the grounds set out in Article 34(2)", the court may set aside if "(a) the making of the award was induced or
-- affected by fraud or corruption; or (b) a breach of the rules of natural justice occurred in connection with the making of the
-- award by which the rights of any party have been prejudiced."
-- A mistake of law or fact is in neither list. (s 25(b) separately says an arbitrator is not liable for "any mistake in law, fact or
-- procedure".)
DECLARE Ground IS ONE OF
    `fraud or corruption`
    `a breach of natural justice that prejudiced a party`
    `a party's incapacity`
    `an invalid arbitration agreement`
    `no proper notice or inability to present the case`
    `a decision beyond the submission`
    `a tribunal or procedure not as agreed`
    `the award is not yet binding or was set aside where made`
    `the dispute is not arbitrable in Singapore`
    `a conflict with Singapore public policy`
    `a mistake of law`
    `a mistake of fact`

GIVEN `the ground` IS A Ground
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the award may be set aside on the ground` IF
    CONSIDER `the ground`
    WHEN `the award is not yet binding or was set aside where made` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a mistake of law` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a mistake of fact` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- Article 34(3): an application "may not be made after three months have elapsed from the date on which the party making that
-- application had received the award or, if a request had been made under Article 33, from the date on which that request had been
-- disposed of by the arbitral tribunal." Months are counted as a number; treating exactly 3 months as still in time is an inference.
GIVEN `months since the award was received` IS A NUMBER, `an Article 33 request was made` IS A BOOLEAN, `months since that request was disposed of` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a setting-aside application is in time` IF
    IF `an Article 33 request was made`
    THEN `months since that request was disposed of` AT MOST 3
    ELSE `months since the award was received` AT MOST 3

§§ `31 -- refusing enforcement of a foreign award`

-- s 31(1): enforcement "in any of the cases mentioned in subsections (2) and (4) may be refused but not otherwise." (2): the person
-- resisting must prove (a) incapacity; (b) an invalid agreement; (c) no proper notice or inability to present the case; (d) a
-- decision beyond the submission; (e) composition or procedure not as agreed or, failing agreement, not per the law of the place of
-- arbitration; (f) "the award has not yet become binding on the parties ... or has been set aside or suspended by a competent
-- authority of the country in which, or under the law of which, the award was made." (4): the court may refuse if (a) the subject
-- matter is not arbitrable under Singapore law or (b) enforcement "would be contrary to the public policy of Singapore".
-- Fraud and natural justice are not listed in s 31 as such; whether they reach s 31 through (2)(c) or (4)(b) the text does not say,
-- so this naive encoding answers FALSE for them.
GIVEN `the ground` IS A Ground
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `enforcement of a foreign award may be refused on the ground` IF
    CONSIDER `the ground`
    WHEN `fraud or corruption` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a breach of natural justice that prejudiced a party` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a mistake of law` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a mistake of fact` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE
